Yes. I would make the project much tighter than the previous version:

> **Benchmark 10 literature-derived agent architectures under one controlled trading environment → identify which components actually help → evolve new architectures from those components → freeze the winner → demonstrate it in Alpaca paper trading.**

That gives you a defensible research claim rather than “we threw lots of agents at markets.”

Your current CoGym already has most of the experimental substrate: deterministic `TradingWorld`, point-in-time `MarketPacket`, immutable `Episode`, `AgentSpec`, deterministic scoring, team/social/persistence experiments, successive-halving evolution, and a hash-chained `EventLedger`.

# 1. The exact research claim

The final submission should be able to say:

> We selected ten representative architectures from published agent and financial-agent research: ReAct, Reflexion, FinMem, FinAgent, FinCon, TradingAgents, Multi-Agent Debate, Mixture-of-Agents, Magentic-One and HedgeAgents.
>
> We reimplemented them behind a common architecture interface, controlled for model, information, tools and inference budget, and evaluated them on deterministic regime-shift worlds and point-in-time historical market replay.
>
> We then treated agent architecture as an evolvable computational graph and searched beyond the ten human-designed systems using ideas from GPTSwarm, AFlow and ADAS.
>
> Validated experimental findings were stored in HydraDB and exposed to the architecture proposer, while secret evaluation remained sealed.
>
> The final architecture was frozen before forward Alpaca paper evaluation.

That's a legitimately interesting experiment.

