# 2. The ten seed architectures

These should be the canonical `ARCH-00` through `ARCH-09`.

| ID | Architecture                | Literature basis                | Core hypothesis                                                         | Reuse                           |
| -- | --------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| A0 | **ReAct Solo**              | ReAct, arXiv:2210.03629         | One tool-using reasoning agent may already be enough                    | `ysymyth/ReAct`                 |
| A1 | **Reflexion Solo**          | Reflexion, arXiv:2303.11366     | Outcome reflection + episodic lessons improves sequential decisions     | implement small adapter         |
| A2 | **FinMem**                  | FinMem, arXiv:2311.13743        | Layered temporal memory beats raw history                               | `wilfrid51/FINMEM`              |
| A3 | **FinAgent**                | FinAgent, arXiv:2402.18485      | multimodal evidence + fast/slow reflection improves adaptation          | `DVampire/FinAgent`             |
| A4 | **FinCon**                  | FinCon, arXiv:2407.06567        | manager hierarchy + selective verbal reinforcement improves learning    | FINSABER adapter + paper        |
| A5 | **TradingAgents**           | TradingAgents, arXiv:2412.20138 | specialist analysts + bull/bear debate + risk review improves decisions | `TauricResearch/TradingAgents`  |
| A6 | **Adversarial Debate**      | MAD, arXiv:2305.19118           | opposing hypotheses prevent premature convergence                       | `Skytliang/Multi-Agents-Debate` |
| A7 | **Mixture-of-Agents**       | MoA, arXiv:2406.04692           | independent proposals + aggregation exploit diversity                   | `togethercomputer/MoA`          |
| A8 | **Dynamic Orchestrator**    | Magentic-One, arXiv:2411.04468  | only invoke specialists when useful                                     | `microsoft/autogen`             |
| A9 | **Heterogeneous Committee** | HedgeAgents, arXiv:2502.13165   | agents with genuinely different objectives outperform generic analysts  | reproduce architecture          |

These cover fundamentally different hypotheses rather than ten cosmetically different prompts.

ReAct establishes the tool-using single-agent control. ([arXiv][1]) Reflexion adds verbal outcome-driven learning. ([arXiv][2]) FinMem specifically introduces layered temporal financial memory. ([arXiv][3]) FinAgent adds multimodal market intelligence and dual-level reflection. ([arXiv][4]) FinCon adds manager/analyst hierarchy and conceptual verbal reinforcement. ([arXiv][5])

TradingAgents gives you the explicit trading-firm architecture—specialist analysts, bull/bear research debate, risk management and portfolio approval—and its maintained repository is already LangGraph-based. ([arXiv][6]) MAD gives you adversarial debate specifically intended to overcome self-reflection's tendency to get stuck in an initial position. ([arXiv][7]) MoA provides a clean parallel-proposer/aggregator design whose reference implementation is tiny. ([arXiv][8]) Magentic-One gives dynamic orchestrator-driven delegation and replanning. ([arXiv][9]) HedgeAgents contributes heterogeneous specialists and manager-mediated coordination rather than interchangeable analyst agents. ([arXiv][10])

